WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.149
"The Healthiest Way to Drink Coffee"

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.040
As I explained
in my last video,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.240
chlorogenic acid,
the main antioxidant in coffee,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.400
enhances autophagy
in human cells,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.640
which may explain why coffee
is linked to a longer life.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.880
If everyone drank three cups
(720 ml) of coffee a day,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.800
millions of healthy
years of life

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.560
could potentially be saved
every year

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.560
in the United States alone.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.000
Which coffee has
the most chlorogenic acid?

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.720
More than a hundred coffees,
espressos, cappuccinos,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.720
and instant coffees
have been tested,

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.080
and chlorogenic acid contents
varied by more than 30-fold.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.360
Interestingly, the major
contributor to this wide range

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.640
was the coffee
purchased from Starbucks,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.520
which had an extremely low
antioxidant content.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.120
This may be because Starbucks
roasts its beans so dark,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.120
it destroys it.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.720
The more you roast coffee,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.520
the lower
the antioxidant levels fall.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.520
Caffeine is relatively stable
to heat,

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.960
but a dark roast may wipe out
nearly 90 percent

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.680
of the chlorogenic acid content
of the beans.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.160
However, the difference
between a medium-light roast

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.720
and a medium roast
did not appear to matter,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.840
at least when it came
to boosting

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.320
the total antioxidant status
in people's bloodstream

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.080
after drinking them.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.560
Soy milk in coffee
appears to be fine,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.720
but adding dairy milk
or creamer

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.800
seems to cut
the bioavailability

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.480
of chlorogenic acid
down by about 40 percent.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.360
The freeze-drying
and spray-drying processes

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.040
used to make instant coffee

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.880
don't appear
to significantly affect

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.400
chlorogenic acid levels,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.920
but the method of preparing
fresh coffee does.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.480
Brewed coffee has higher
chlorogenic acid content

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.000
than espresso,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.560
presumably due
to the longer contact time

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.320
between the water
and coffee grounds

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.720
and the greater
ultimate volume.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.480
No differences were found
between cold brew or hot.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.640
The brewing method
can also impact

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.480
our cholesterol levels.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.160
The culprits are two compounds,
cafestol and kahweol,

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.200
which are found in the fines
of the coffee grounds,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.640
tiny particles that get trapped
in the paper filter,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.640
which explains
why filtered coffee

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.360
doesn't raise cholesterol
as much as boiled,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.080
French press,
or Turkish coffee does.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.160
If you don't have
optimal cholesterol levels,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.040
you should consider sticking
to paper-filtered coffee

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.800
or using instant coffee

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.960
which also lacks
these compounds.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.480
Mesh filters are
likely insufficient,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.400
as even paper filters
sporting "micro perforations"

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.040
allow more of the
cholesterol-raising compounds

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.120
to slip through,
particularly when the coffee

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.080
is ground especially fine.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.880
And indeed,
those drinking unfiltered coffee

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.800
were found to have
higher mortality rates

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.120
than those drinking
filtered coffee,

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.600
though any kind of coffee
may be better

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.200
than no coffee at all,
at least for women.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.520
These findings
led some to bemoan

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.160
the growing popularity
of unfiltered brews

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.280
from capsule coffee machines,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.040
but if you cut them open,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.120
you can see
the little plastic cups

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.440
actually do have
a paper filter inside.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.440
However, capsule coffee

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.000
ends up with more estrogenic
chemicals in it,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.840
as one would expect
from exposing

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.720
nearly any sort of plastic
to hot enough water,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.400
but the levels were found low

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.760
compared to established
safety guidelines.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.200
If coffee can extend our lives
by boosting autophagy,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.120
potentially preventing
hundreds of thousands

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.840
of cases or deaths
from various diseases,

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.920
should everyone be drinking it?

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:37.520
Is there anyone
who should avoid coffee?

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.864
We'll find out next.

